The Azure Well-Architected Framework provides a structured approach for designing and evaluating cloud workloads. It helps organizations make informed architectural decisions instead of focusing only on whether an application can be deployed successfully.

The major principles include:

* Reliability
* Security
* Cost Optimization
* Operational Excellence
* Performance Efficiency

For example, an e-commerce application may use multiple Availability Zones for reliability, Microsoft Entra ID and RBAC for security, autoscaling for performance, and Azure cost management practices to control expenditure.

A real-life use case is a company reviewing an existing production application before a major seasonal sales event. The architecture team evaluates whether the workload can handle increased traffic while remaining secure, reliable, and cost-effective.

Using these principles helps organizations build Azure workloads that are aligned with both technical requirements and business objectives.
